Why, When, and How to Use ArrayDeque Instead of Stack

Java developers are strongly encouraged to use ArrayDeque instead of Stack for LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) operations.

1. Why we need to use ArrayDeque over Stack

The java.util.Stack class is a legacy class introduced in Java 1.0. It is considered an architectural mistake in Java for two main reasons:
1. Unnecessary Synchronization Overhead: Stack extends Vector, meaning every single method (push, pop, peek) is synchronized using internal locks. If you are working in a single-threaded environment (which is 99% of standard stack operations), this synchronization introduces a massive, unnecessary performance penalty. ArrayDeque is not synchronized and is significantly faster.
2. Broken Object-Oriented Design: Because Stack inherits from Vector, it accidentally exposes methods that a stack should never have. For example, you can call stack.add(0, "Element") or stack.remove(5). This violates the basic principle of a Stack, which should only allow you to interact with the top element. ArrayDeque forces you to use strict Double-Ended Queue principles.

2. When we need to use ArrayDeque

You should choose ArrayDeque whenever you need:
• A Stack (LIFO): Simulating Undo/Redo operations, parsing syntax/brackets, or tracking history.
• A Queue (FIFO): Simulating a waiting line or implementing Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithms.
• Single-threaded performance: High-performance, local data structures where thread-safety is handled elsewhere or not needed.
Note: If you genuinely need a thread-safe stack across multiple threads, do not use Stack. Instead, use LinkedBlockingDeque or wrap your deque using Collections.synchronizedDeque().

3. How to use ArrayDeque as a Stack

To use ArrayDeque as a stack, you map the old Stack methods to their Deque equivalents.

Stack Action	    Legacy Stack Method	        Modern ArrayDeque Method
Add item to top	            push(e)	                            push(e)
Remove item from top	pop()	                                    pop()
View item at top	            peek()	                                peek() or peekFirst()
Check if empty              	empty()                                 	isEmpty()
